For anyone who cares, this is because the quotes mark off everything
inside them as one token; it's used to deal with possible spaces in
path and filenames. So, unless a file named "NetHackW.exe -u wizard -D"
exists in "C:\path\to\nethack\", the first technique wouldn't work (and
almost certainly wouldn't do what you wanted it to even if it did).

    c:\path\nethack -u fred              : 3 tokens
    c:\weird path\nethack -u fred        : 4 tokens (space in directory
name)
    "c:\weird path\nethack" -u fred      : 3 tokens (quotes > space)
    "c:\weird path\nethack -u fred"      : 1 token (quotes capture
everything)



